in case you haven't heard the good news  both Microsoft edge and WebKit Safari  are now supporting service workers in  their preview releases this is a huge  leap forward and sets up 2018 to be the  year of progressive web apps with that  being said what I want to show you today  is how to handle touch events that only  happen on mobile devices then use  angular to create animations in response  to these events as you can see here we  run a different animation on this  material card every time the user swipes  in a different direction if you're new  here make sure to subscribe and I  recommend that you follow along with the  source code at angular firebase comm and  while you're at it  I also recommend checking out Paul  Holidays YouTube channel and all the  awesome angular content he's creating  over there I'm going to kick this  episode off by building a brand new  angular app then we have a couple of  dependencies to add the first one being  hammer J S which is used to add support  for mobile touch events to your  progressive web app including rotate  pinch press pan tap and swipe then you  can install it in your app by running  NPM install hammer J s Flags save then  once installed we're going to go into  the main TS file and make sure to import  hammer J s there that'll make the events  globally available throughout your app  in this demo I'm using angular material  but that part is completely optional you  can follow the install instructions on  the official repo the important part is  that you install angular animations  which is included in the angular mono  repo so you can just install it with NPM  and then make sure to include it inside  of your app module so you should have  browser animations module inside of the  imports array we're going to be modeling  our animations after the animate CSS  library which is one of the most popular  libraries on github the library itself  is just a collection of CSS keyframe  animations so all we have to do is port  those over to angular so we can use it  with angular's browser animations module  we don't actually need to install this  package in our angular project instead  we're just going to copy and paste the  CSS keyframes over to the angular  animations keyframes the end result is  that we'll be able to trigger animations  natively in angular this will give us  fine-grained control over the animation  lifecycle making it much easier to  manage the animations state as our app  grows more complex this can be extremely  valuable if you're building something  like swipeable tinder cards or if you  just want to  optimize the animations for your mobile  users now that we have all of our  dependencies in place let's go ahead and  create a new component called hammer  card the first thing we'll do is set up  the component type script so we're going  to import the necessary tools from  angular animations then I'm going to  import a keyframes file that has not yet  been created but we're going to do that  in the next step then inside the  component decorator we'll go ahead and  create an animations array and this is  where we define each of the animations  as well as the animation trigger so the  trigger is called card animator and  every time that trigger changes it will  transition to a different state so to  define a transition we use the wild card  which means any state that transitions  to wobble will animate in 1000  milliseconds then we'll tell it to  animate with the wobble keyframes that  we're going to define in that key frames  file we're going to follow the same  exact steps for each of our animations I  have 6 in total in this demo so we can  just add those line by line inside of  this animation trigger after that we can  go into the component and we'll define a  variable to hold the animation state to  trigger an animation all we have to do  is define the animation state as a  string that matches one of the  animations that we defined above to do  that I'll create a method called start  animation that takes a state as an  argument then I'm wrapping it in an if  statement so we don't have two  animations trying to run at the same  time and after each animation completes  we want to reset the animation state  back to an empty string so I'll go ahead  and create another method to handle that  the next step is to define the keyframes  that we referenced earlier so create a  new file called keyframes TS and you can  put this file wherever you want and it  can be shared throughout your  application first we'll import keyframes  in style from angular animations and  then I'm going to pull up animate CSS  side-by-side so you can see how this is  done all we're doing in this file is  exporting variables that define the  keyframes that we're going to use in the  animation each element in the array is a  style that represents a relative point  in time in the CSS keyframes to the  right the relative point in time is the  percentage but in angular it's  represented by the offset property  in this object the basic process is that  we convert that percentage to a decimal  and add it to the offset property and  then we're also going to copy and paste  over the CSS styles from each keyframe  it's a somewhat tedious process and  eventually angular is going to have its  own CSS parser but for now this is the  best way to go about it to keep this  brief I'm only going to show you this  first animation here but you can find  all of the animations in the github  project for this video if we look a  little closer at the completed keyframes  you can see we start at point two and  then we ended an offset of point eight  keyframes seem to be ordered ascending  based on their offset and the last  keyframe should be no greater than one  if you remember earlier we set our  transition to be 1000 milliseconds so  it's going to cycle through these  keyframes based on that duration of time  with the keyframes in place let's jump  over to the component HTML and start  putting this together so we start with  the material card then we define the  animation trigger by calling at Card  animator with the animation state then  angular calls a done event when the  animation is finished so when it's  finished we're going to reset the  animation state back to an empty string  now we have a whole bunch of hammer j/s  gesture touch events that we can start  playing with first we'll listen to the  swipe left event when the user swipes  left we'll call the start animation  function and pass it the slide out left  animation for swipe right we'll use the  zoom out right animation then we'll also  add swipe up and swipe down but these  ones are going to need some additional  configuration which we'll get to in the  next step then we can go ahead and add  the angular logo and some default text  to our card then we'll set up a couple  material buttons to trigger animations  on a button click or tap with hammer J  ass a tap is similar to a click but  optimized for mobile devices and can  provide some additional metadata for pre  ding custom events at this point you can  open up chrome dev tools and run the  mobile application emulator and you  should see all the animations running  like you would expect so that's pretty  cool but I still want to show you how  you can create your own custom events as  well as customize the behavior of hammer  J s the swipe up and swipe down events  are disabled by default but if we want  to enable them we can import hammer J s  into our app  and then angular has a hammer gesture  config built into platform browser you  create your own hammer config class and  then you give it an overrides object  which gives you access to the full  hammer jsapi you can check out all the  options in the hammer Docs but for now  we just need to set the swipe event to  Direction all then to use this config we  need to go down to providers and tell  angular to use our custom class that'll  get the swipe up and swipe down events  working but I want to take this one step  further and create my own custom events  this is a perfect use case for an  angular directive which I'm going to  call hammer time then to create custom  events and angular you're going to need  host listener output and event emitter  first we'll use the output decorator on  a property called double tap which is  equal to a new event emitter and double  taps going to happen whenever the user  taps twice on an object and then we'll  do the same thing for triple tap which  is the same exact thing but when a user  taps three times to create these custom  events will first listen to the main tap  event coming from hammer Jas and hammer  gives us the tap count for each event so  we can just wrap that in an if statement  and if the tap count equals two then we  know that the user has double tap then  inside the if block will call double tap  omit this event now the process is  exactly the same for triple tap just  looking for the tap count to equal three  now our director is ready to put to use  so we'll go back to our component HTML  and add the directive to whichever  button we want to listen to these custom  events so you can see here we add  another button with the hammer time  directive and then we can listen to the  double tap event in parentheses then we  can run the same start animation  function and pass whichever animation we  want to use there if we go back to our  app and tap the button once you can see  nothing happens but if we double tap  than it does the jello animation that's  just barely scratching the surface of  angular animations and hammer j/s  if this video helped you please like and  subscribe and if you want to take your  app to the next level consider becoming  a pro subscriber at angular firebase  comm you'll get access to a support  network designed to help you get your  app built and shipped into production as  well as a free copy my book and access  to exclusive content every week thanks  for watching and I'll see you soon  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

